{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not besiege them, and you shall not provoke them.</b> It is not said here, 'and you shall not provoke them to war,' in the manner stated above concerning Moab — because generic provocation without war was not forbidden regarding Moab, but regarding Ammon He forbade even bare provocation without war. This is because Moab hired Balaam against Israel, and we do not find that Ammon had any part in this; and even though his actions were of no avail, nevertheless he caused Israel distress. And furthermore, Moab also had a part in the licentiousness, and we do not find this among the children of Ammon — for although on account of their not coming forth with bread and water they caused Israel to engage in licentiousness, as is explained above in the pericope of Phinehas (Numbers 25:17), nevertheless we do not find that they made their daughters freely available. And perhaps this traces back to their root, for also in the incident of Lot's daughters their mother was somewhat modest."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:19.714825255Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}